  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\nA passing disturbance is expected tonight as a shortwave rounds\nan eastern trough. Mid-level ascent will invoke showers, with\nthe only chance of accumulations north of I-76. Areas with a\n>30% chance of 0.1\" only linger for northern Venango County and\nForest County With temperatures in the upper 30s / low 40s, \nthis will likely fall as rain, save a chance of graupel should \nany showers be more robust.\n\nThis shortwave will enhance the synoptic wind gradient across \nthe region. With most areas expected to be below a low-to-mid \nlevel inversion, winds will not be particularly strong, but for \neastern Tucker County, there is a chance that ridgetops may \nbriefly poke into the inversion, prompting the issuance of a \nWind Advisory from 7pm to 10am Monday. Most winds >45mph, if\nany, are expected on peaks above 4kft, with chances in \nDavis/Thomas of those same winds at a more marginal 40% to 60%.\n\nAfter this shortwave passage, ridging and a temperature rises \nare expected through mid-week. High temperatures will reach \n5-10F above normal Tuesday into Wednesday.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2... \nA turn to troughing is favored Wednesday into Thursday, though \nensemble clustering is still deviant on the trough depth. Some \ntake it from Quebec back to the Great Plains, while others take \ninto the upper midwest. This will in-tun, force where the \nsurface cyclogenesis response. By Thursday into Friday, most \nscenarios fall into 3 camps:\n\n1) Weak and eastern trough axis. These scenarios are\nconsiderably drier with the trough mostly passing as a\nshortwave, leading to little rain accumulation and no snow\naccumulation. Temperatures are \"warmer than the ensemble mean\"\nbut highs still dip into the 40s late week.\n\n2) Deeper, northeast trough axis. This brings more rain and even\nsome snow (most likely for high terrain).This is primarily\nfocused Thursday into Friday. \n\n3) Deeper, midwest trough axis. This also bring more rain and\nperhaps some snow, but peak accumulations are delayed by 18 to\n24 hours, mostly on Friday into Saturday. \n\nScenarios 2/3 show the potential for \"at least\" advisory level\nsnow in the high terrain of West Virginia, with the terrain of\nPennsylvania more marginal. Chances of any snow accumulation in\nthe Pittsburgh metro are still low. There is only a 20% chance \nof any accumulation, and a <5% chance of an inch.",
